Dystonia or Tetany (Undifferentiated Finding)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Sustained involuntary muscle contractions, Twisting patterned postures, Sensory trick phenomena

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, distressing, and functionally impairing involuntary muscle spasms of the face and upper extremities, causing severe anxiety.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young adult presenting with acute, sustained, abnormal twisting postures of the hands and face, accompanied by perioral tingling. Urgent medical assessment was critical to differentiate between a benign medication-induced acute dystonic reaction, severe hypocalcemic tetany, or a new central neurological event, and to administer immediate, specific pharmacological reversal agents prior to morning services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands keep locking into these weird claws, my mouth is tingling, and my neck keeps pulling sharply to the right."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute onset of bizarre, involuntary muscle contractions. Timeline: Patient noticed mild perioral tingling and acral paresthesias 3 hours ago; rapidly progressed over the last hour into sustained, intermittent muscle contractions causing abnormal, twisting physical distortions. Reports carpopedal spasms characterized by severe flexion of the wrists, extension of the interphalangeal joints, and adduction of the thumbs. The neck periodically pulls into a sustained rightward torticollis. Patient notes that touching the side of their face (sensory trick/geste antagoniste) provides a temporary, palpable reduction in the neck spasm severity. Worsening of involuntary movements occurs when trying to actively hold an object.
Functional Status: Highly impaired manual dexterity; unable to hold a phone or drink a glass of water.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound anxiety and panic secondary to loss of bodily control.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, displaying intermittent torticollis and bilateral carpal spasms.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals normal global motor strength in periods between spasms, intact sensation, and brisk, symmetrical 3+ hyperreflexia. Cranial nerves grossly intact. Observation confirms rhythmic, directional dystonic posturing of the hands (carpopedal spasm). Chvostek's phenomenon is strongly positive: tapping the facial nerve just anterior to the earlobe elicits distinct ipsilateral facial muscle twitching. Trousseau's phenomenon induced: inflating the sphygmomanometer above systolic pressure for 2 minutes provokes a severe carpal spasm. Pupils are equal and reactive. No rigidity or fever to suggest Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome.
Environmental Observations: Several empty blister packs of a recently prescribed anti-emetic (Metoclopramide) visible on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient confirms taking high doses of Metoclopramide over the last 48 hours for a presumed viral gastroenteritis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Dystonic Reaction (likely secondary to Metoclopramide) with concurrent hyperventilation-induced Tetany. - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Hypocalcemia (Severe) - Ruled out as the primary isolated cause due to the highly specific localized torticollis and the clear medication history, though the hyperventilation is exacerbating tetanic features (Chvostek's/Trousseau's).

Seizure (Focal Motor) - Ruled out by fully preserved consciousness, absence of a post-ictal state, and the sustained, directional nature of the contractions rather than rhythmic clonic jerking.

Stroke (Acute Ischemic) - Ruled out by the bilateral, fluctuating nature of the spasms, positive sensory trick phenomenon, and absence of unilateral weakness, facial droop, or speech aphasia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a highly distressing constellation of acute dystonic posturing (torticollis) and tetanic signs (carpopedal spasms, positive Chvostek's), accompanied by significant anxiety and hyperventilation. The critical discovery was the recent, high-dose ingestion of a dopamine antagonist (Metoclopramide). Postponing clinical evaluation would have subjected the patient to hours of terrifying, uncontrollable muscle spasms and escalating panic. Urgent after-hours assessment safely ruled out a primary central neurological event (stroke/seizure), rapidly identified the pharmacological trigger, and permitted the immediate, definitive administration of an anticholinergic reversal agent, rapidly resolving the crisis in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Benztropine 2mg STAT. Symptoms largely resolved within 20 minutes. Advised immediate, total cessation of Metoclopramide.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Breathe slowly and rhythmically into a paper bag for 3 minutes if the tingling in the lips returns. Consequence: Re-breathing carbon dioxide corrects the respiratory alkalosis caused by panic hyperventilation, rapidly stopping the terrifying tingling and hand cramping (negative reinforcement).
Action: Discard the offending anti-emetic medication immediately. Consequence: Complete prevention of a recurrent, agonizing dystonic episode tomorrow (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 24-48 hours. A brief course of oral anticholinergics may be prescribed if symptoms rebound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden difficulty breathing, swelling of the tongue or throat, high fever, or severe muscle rigidity throughout the entire body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

